Speaker 1: Yeah. I mean, listen, there's obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:46.000
hypocrisy. There's no question about it. Also,&nbsp;
sometimes public health guidelines are supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.320
at a governmental level and individuals assess&nbsp;
their own risk and they do different things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.840
I mean, OK, you know, it doesn't make it not&nbsp;
hypocritical. It just is something that happens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.240
I don't know about the event. You know, sometimes&nbsp;
people go to I've gone to a couple of events where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.440
everybody's got to be vaccinated and gets a test&nbsp;
on the way in. And then like, I went to a dinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.880
with 10 people like that last month, and that&nbsp;
seemed quite reasonable. And and we don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.400
what the testing requirement is. I'm about to go&nbsp;
to a wedding where everybody's going to be tested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.440
So I don't know. I mean, of course, there's some&nbsp;
level of hypocrisy. And then at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.800
I think a lot of the stuff is in the details.
Speaker 9: OK. I mean, do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.760
like at a certain point, the personal&nbsp;
responsibility will be kind of like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.880
data ultimately is the thing that&nbsp;
people want to take upon themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.400
outside of, like these mass mandates?
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.160
going to go more and more in that direction. And&nbsp;
like Dr. Isaac Bogoch last week told us, once we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.400
